---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Navigation from "../../components/Navigation.astro";
import Link from "../../components/Link.astro";
import Inline from "../../components/Inline.astro";
import Ex1 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex1.astro";
import Ex2 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex2.astro";
import Ex3 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex3.astro";
import Ex4 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex4.astro";
import Ex5 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex5.astro";
import Ex6 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex6.astro";
import Ex7 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex7.astro";
import Ex8 from "../../components/blogs/code-as-html-in-neovim/Ex8.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <Navigation />
  <article class="font-plex text-article text-lg">
    <div class="container max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4">
      <div
        class="font-plex font-bold text-center text-2xl md:text-3xl text-primary mb-8 pb-2 pt-8 border-b-2 border-b-border-dark border-dashed"
      >
        <h2>Transform Code Snippets to HTML in Neovim</h2>
        <div class="text-gray-600 text-center text-sm py-1">
          PUBLISHED 12-05-2025 / UPDATED 12-05-2025
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="md:mx-10">
        <p>
          I am not sure how people start blogs or if there's some general
          pattern to follow, but I'm going to give up trying to be clever and
          just dive straight in. Over Thanksgiving break I decided I wanted to
          start a blog but wasn't sure where to start. I had started with
          creating this site and added a projects section while styling
          everything after a colorscheme I had created in Neovim -- <Link
            href="https://github.com/vertexE/synth.nvim">synth.nvim</Link
          >. But before I could start blogging, I wanted an easy utility to
          transform code snippets from my Neovim editor into HTML I could paste
          into an astro component.
        </p>
        <br />
        <p>
          If you want to skip reading through the how and go straight to the
          source, see my <Link
            href="https://github.com/vertexE/init.lua/blob/trunk/lua/treesitter/extract.lua"
            >Neovim config here</Link
          >.
        </p>
        <br />
        <h3 class="font-plex text-xl text-light font-bold mb-1">Plan</h3>
        <p>
          Before I started coding, I found a couple Neovim apis I would need to
          use.
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-2 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/lua.html#_lua-module:-vim.inspector"
                >vim.inspect_pos</Link
              > - how to fetch the highlight groups of a node using a row, col position
              within a buffer
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#_vim.treesitter"
                >vim.treesitter</Link
              > - what would parse a code file and tell me where different nodes
              (and thus highlights) start and stop
            </li>
          </ul>

          For <Inline>vim.inspect_pos</Inline>, you can use this in practically
          the same way as <Inline>:Inspect</Inline>. For example, if you created
          a scratch buffer in nvim, added the following
        </p>

        <div class="py-4">
          <Ex1 />
        </div>

        <p>Then run <Inline>:%lua</Inline> you'll get the following output</p>

        <div class="py-4">
          <Ex2 />
        </div>

        <p>
          The above gives us a reliable method to extract the <Inline
            >hl_group_link</Inline
          >, which in turn allows us to use <Inline>vim.api.nvim_get_hl</Inline>
          to get the fg/bg values. Something to keep in mind, the <Inline
            >vim.api.nvim_get_hl</Inline
          > function returns fg/bg as number values, not hex values, so we'll also
          want to convert that to hex to make it easier to use in css.
        </p>

        <br />

        <p>
          For treesitter, that I relied more on online examples and Claude to
          understand the right functions to call, but the actual idea is quite
          simple.
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 py-2 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#vim.treesitter.get_parser()"
                >vim.treesitter.get_parser</Link
              > - obviously, it gets the parser for the buffer
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#treesitter-languagetree"
                >vim.treesitter.LanguageTree:parse()</Link
              > - before we can walk the tree, we parse the content in the buffer,
              which returns a list of trees (I only ever saw 1 tree returned, so
              I always used that).
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#TSTree%3Aroot()"
                >TSTree:root()</Link
              > - get the top node to start
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#TSNode%3Aiter_children()"
                >TSNode:iter_children()</Link
              > - using this func to recursively walk the tree, looking for leaf
              nodes that are in the line range I want.
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#TSNode%3Arange()"
                >TSNode:range()</Link
              > - to get <Inline>start_row, start_col, end_row, end_col</Inline>
              of the node
            </li>

            <li>
              <Link
                href="https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter.html#vim.treesitter.get_node_text()"
                >vim.treesitter.get_node_text</Link
              > - for the text inside the node
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          With these treesitter functions + vim.inspect_pos, we can now build
          out our utility module.
        </p>
        <br />
        <h3 class="font-plex text-xl text-light font-bold mb-1">
          M.highlights
        </h3>
        <p>
          I'm breaking this problem down into 2 major functions.
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 py-2 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <Inline>M.highlights</Inline>, will handle everything we've
              covered so far, assembling a list of items to represent different
              treesitter nodes including the fg/bg highlights in hex.
            </li>
            <li>
              <Inline>M.as_html</Inline> will convert what the above function returns
              to html, ready to paste into any .html file.
            </li>
          </ul>
          For <Inline>M.highlights</Inline>, I came up with the following
          <div class="py-4">
            <Ex3 />
          </div>
          The approach is straightforward. We parse the tree as discussed above and
          then recursively walk the nodes. For grabbing the highlight groups, that
          will be done inside walk
          <div class="py-4">
            <Ex4 />
          </div>
          An aside on extracting out text. I've noticed sometimes <Inline
            >vim.treesitter.get_node_text</Inline
          > does not work perfectly. I'm not sure why but when I called it on a
          <Inline>(line_comment)</Inline> node for rust (see <Inline
            >:InspectTree</Inline
          >) and TreeSitter wouldn't extract the actual comment, only the marker
          <Inline>//</Inline>. Because of the issue, I added a special
          condition. I haven't noticed any other issues so far (using
          JS/TS/Golang/Rust/Lua), but it's possible other edge cases exist not
          covered.
          <div class="py-4">
            <Ex5 />
          </div>
          To support the above, I also added a small function to use the <Inline
            >hl_group_link</Inline
          > and fetch the hl_group fg/bg values. After that, it will convert the
          numerical values to their hex representation for ease of use in the next
          part.
          <div class="py-4">
            <Ex6 />
          </div>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now we have covered <Inline>M.highlights</Inline> we'll start looking at
          how we can convert the code to html. See <Link
            href="https://github.com/vertexE/init.lua/blob/trunk/lua/treesitter/extract.lua#L27"
            >here</Link
          > for the full source code of what we just went over.
        </p>
        <br />
        <h3 class="font-plex text-xl text-light font-bold mb-1">M.as_html</h3>
        <p>
          Now that we have a list of treesitter nodes with their fg/bg values,
          we need to transform this into html that we can then paste in an astro
          file. To best do this, we'll want to
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 py-2 space-y-2">
            <li>
              Create a <Inline>&lt;styles&gt;</Inline> section to list out color
              classes
            </li>
            <li>Parse each node token, constructing lines of text</li>
            <li>
              Correctly add spacing to the start of lines and between treesitter
              nodes
            </li>
            <li>Add a button to copy the code content to the clipboard</li>
          </ol>

          Both adding the fg/bg colors as font text color to the css classes and
          parsing the list of nodes into lines is very simple, so I'll skip
          showing that. The last item to add a button is also simple. We can add
          a <Inline>&lt;script&gt;</Inline> tag to the bottom of the content and
          then add a button in the corner. The third item, <strong
            >adding spaces at start and between tokens is more interesting</strong
          >
        </p>
        <br />
        <p>
          Solving the whitespace before the start of any text on each line was
          easy. I checked if we had moved to a new line and then added
          whitespace to match the <Inline>item.col</Inline> value.

          <div class="py-4">
            <Ex7 />
          </div>

          This snippet got me spaces at the start, but how should I add spaces
          between? Eventually I realized I could use the <Inline
            >item.end_col</Inline
          > of the previous treesitter node and compare that to the <Inline
            >item.col</Inline
          >. If the nodes are right next to each other, then they will be equal
          (<Inline>end_col</Inline> is exclusive index). I can also use the difference
          of the fields to calculate how many spaces to insert between the nodes.

          <div class="py-4">
            <Ex8 />
          </div>
        </p>
        <p>
          And with that we've solved all of the core issues in creating <Inline
            >M.as_html</Inline
          >. I now have a (mostly) reliable way of converting code into html.
        </p>
        <br />
        <h3 class="font-plex text-xl text-light font-bold mb-1">Conclusion</h3>
        <p>
          Overall the code works (<Link
            href="https://github.com/vertexE/init.lua/blob/trunk/lua/treesitter/extract.lua"
            >check it out here!</Link
          >), though edge cases and small bugs still remain. First, Rust
          comments were getting parsed weirdly. The <Inline
            >vim.treesitter.get_node_text</Inline
          > API would return no text other than <Inline>//</Inline>. This wasn't
          too hard to resolve as I simply used
          <Inline>vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines</Inline> and the nodes position to manually
          extract out the code comments. There are also limitations with the approach
          I used. If another plugin is applying highlights inside a treesitter node,
          then those will not be copied. Virtual text is also not supported as that
          will not appear inside a parsed tree from treesitter. Finally, there's
          a lot of cleanup I would like to do, such as converting all of token classes
          to tailwind.
        </p>

        <br />
        <p>
          If you would like to use this yourself, I would suggest not directly
          copying the code I wrote, but instead use the core logic I reviewed
          above and write your own! The one I created still isn't perfect and
          you might find a better approach in the end.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
<Footer />
